Electric vehicles can also use a [ motor in each wheel hub
](/wiki/Wheel_hub_motor "Wheel hub motor") or next to the wheels; this is rare
but claimed to be safer.  [101]  Electric vehicles that lack an [ axle
](/wiki/Axle "Axle") , [ differential ](/wiki/Differential_\(mechanics\)
"Differential \(mechanics\)") , or [ transmission
](/wiki/Transmission_\(mechanics\) "Transmission \(mechanics\)") can have less
drivetrain inertia. Some [ direct current motor ](/wiki/DC_motor "DC motor")
-equipped drag racer EVs have simple two-speed [ manual transmissions
](/wiki/Manual_transmission "Manual transmission") to improve top speed.
[102]  The concept electric supercar [ Rimac Concept One
](/wiki/Rimac_Concept_One "Rimac Concept One") claims it can go from 0–97 km/h
(0–60 mph) in 2.5 seconds. Tesla claims the upcoming [ Tesla Roadster
](/wiki/Tesla_Roadster_\(2020\) "Tesla Roadster \(2020\)") will go 0–60 mph
(0–97 km/h) in 1.9 seconds.  [103]

